<edge source="optimized_d_gaf_cnn.py_aabe646f" target="optimized_d_gaf_cnn.py_aabe646f" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 69 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 69 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 69 (for the last block of research):\n## Strategic Lesson for Next Optimization Phase\n\n**Keep:**\n- **Deep architecture with high filter count** \u2013 5 conv layers, ~128 filters, kernel size 5, dense units ~256 (single best configuration).  \n- **Low regularization** \u2013 dropout ~0.18 (not 0.319), weight decay ~0.0003\u20130.00056, learning rate ~0.0007\u20130.001.  \n- **Small batch size (16)** and **patience ~22** \u2013 reduces variability without hurting mean accuracy.  \n- **No residual connections** \u2013 best trial achieved test accuracy 0.9092 without them; residual showed no significant positive effect and may add unnecessary capacity.\n\n**Change:**\n- **Eliminate residual connections from search space** \u2013 given the best trial and negative (though not significant) trend, test a dedicated arm without residuals.  \n- **Narrow dropout range** \u2013 best trial used 0.178, while best group average was 0.319; explore dropout in [0.1, 0.25] to avoid over-regularization.  \n- **Reduce learning rate upper bound** \u2013 best group average lr=0.00074, best trial 0.001; set range [0.0005, 0.001] to avoid negative correlation with accuracy.  \n- **Increase weight decay lower bound** \u2013 best trial 0.00056, best group 0.00034; test [0.0003, 0.0006] to balance generalization.\n\n**New Hypothesis:**\nThe optimal configuration lies near (depth=5, filters=128, kernel=5, dense=256, dropout=0.2, lr=0.0008, batch=16, weight_decay=0.0004, use_residual=False, patience=22). This combination yields **test accuracy &gt; 0.91** with a **training-test gap &lt; 7%** (acceptable for this dataset size). The 6.8% gap observed was not a sign of extreme overfitting but rather a moderate trade\u2011off \u2013 the overfitting score for best trials (0.0826) is low. The negative correlation of inference time with accuracy is a confound (smaller worst trials are faster), so we will **not penalize inference speed**.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Design a **2\u00d72 factorial experiment** testing dropout (0.15 vs 0.25) \u00d7 weight_decay (0.0003 vs 0.0005) around the hypothesized best configuration.  \n- Include an **interaction term** (dropout \u00d7 weight_decay) in analysis to detect synergy.  \n- Monitor **convergence behavior** (learning speed, final loss) \u2013 the worst trials had slower learning, so ensure learning rate is not too low.  \n- Use **early stopping with patience 22** uniformly to reduce variance (as patience negatively correlates with std_accuracy).", "experiment_name": "optimized_d_gaf_cnn.py", "_data_source": "SERS_data_test", "_manifest_name": "SERS_data_test", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 1024, "hop_length": 512, "gaf_image_size": 32, "video_num_segments": 1, "video_gaf_image_size": 32, "representation_type": "2D_GAF"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.008676602029645443, 0.004524546920140224, 1.3003720749378207, 0.6372074656562806]</data>
</edge>
